WLED
----

LedBalloon drives WLED controllers and reproduces WLED's behaviour, and could
not exist without it.

    https://github.com/wled/WLED
    Copyright (c) 2016 Christian Schwinne and contributors
    Licensed under the EUPL-1.2

The EUPL's compatibility clause permits a derivative work to be licensed under
the GPL-3.0, and that is the route taken here.

Two parts of this project stand in different relationships to WLED, and the
difference is worth stating plainly.

  src/LedBalloon.Core/Effects/

      Implementations of WLED's lighting effects, so that the app can draw on a
      photograph of the house what the controller is about to draw on the house
      itself. How closely these follow WLED's own FX.cpp is not recorded in this
      project's history, and the people who would know cannot now reconstruct it
      with confidence. They are therefore treated as derivative works of WLED.
      That is the conservative reading, it is the one this licence reflects, and
      it costs nothing to be wrong about in this direction.

  everything else

      Interoperability rather than derivation: the JSON API, the audio sync
      packet layout, the custom palette file format and the pixel order on the
      wire. Those are how two programs talk to each other, not a copy of either.


FastLED
-------

WLED's effects are built on FastLED's fixed-point integer maths, and
src/LedBalloon.Core/Effects/FastLed.cs reproduces the parts of it the effects
read - the sine approximation, the 8-bit scaling, the beat generators.

    https://github.com/FastLED/FastLED
    Copyright (c) 2013 FastLED
    Licensed under the MIT licence, which is compatible with the GPL-3.0


Why any of this is copied at all
--------------------------------

The app paints a photograph of the house with the colors the house is about to
show, which means it has to arrive at the same picture the controller does. An
effect that is close but not exact is worse than no preview, because it is a
preview you cannot trust and have no way to check. So the arithmetic is
reproduced rather than approximated, down to a sine that is deliberately a
little wrong in the way the firmware's is.
